  "worked_solution": "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{\u2032}=f(x,y),\\,{y}_{0}=y({x}_{0}),\n$$\n\nthe implicit Euler method with step size $\u0394x$ is given by\n\n$$\n\u0394y={y}_{\\text{new}}^{\u2032}\\cdot \u0394x,\n$$\n\nwith ${y}_{\\text{new}}^{\u2032}=f({x}_{\\text{new}},{y}_{\\text{new}})$ evaluated at the next $x$ and $y$ values.\n\nFirst, since we want to find the value of $y$ at $x=1,$ we will use a step size of\n\n$$\n\u0394x=1-0=1.\n$$\n\nNow, let's proceed with the implicit Euler method. In general, $\u0394y$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\u0394y & ={y}_{\\text{new}}^{\u2032}\\cdot \u0394x \\\\  & =(3{x}_{\\text{new}}{y}_{\\text{new}}+{y}_{\\text{new}})\\cdot 1 \\\\  & ={y}_{\\text{new}}(3{x}_{\\text{new}}+1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute this into ${y}_{\\text{new}}=y+\u0394y$ and solve for the general formula for ${y}_{\\text{new}}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+\u0394y \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+{y}_{\\text{new}}(3{x}_{\\text{new}}+1),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}}-{y}_{\\text{new}}(3{x}_{\\text{new}}+1) & =y \\\\ (1-(3{x}_{\\text{new}}+1)){y}_{\\text{new}} & =y \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =\\frac{y}{1-(3{x}_{\\text{new}}+1)} \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =\\frac{y}{3{x}_{\\text{new}}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, in the first step, the new $x$ value is ${x}_{\\text{new}}=x+\u0394x=0+1=1.$ Hence, the new $y$ value is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =\\frac{y}{3{x}_{\\text{new}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3\\cdot 1} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{3} \\\\  & \u2248-0.67,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $2$ decimal places.\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $y(1)\u2248-0.67.$",
